WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:04.509
To start this course on networking, let's
look at four basic groups of terms and definitions

00:00:04.509 --> 00:00:06.189
you will want to know going forward.

00:00:06.189 --> 00:00:10.780
This first involves internet versus intranet
versus extranet.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.940
The internet is the world's largest public
network and offers many ways to connect to

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.590
a vast amount of information.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:20.230
I am on the internet right now, looking at
a website, for example.

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:22.570
You may have also heard of the term intranet.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:27.720
An intranet is a website that is internal
to a business and not seen by the public.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.510
If you have an intranet and decide to allow
some members from outside an organization

00:00:31.510 --> 00:00:36.310
into the intranet, you technically have an
extranet.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:41.100
There are two basic types of networks for
people to know about: peer-to-peer and client-server.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.170
Most home and small office networks are peer-to-peer
networks where there is no central device

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:48.480
managing and storing users, files, and folders.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.559
In essence, every device on a peer-to-peer
network is its own server because every device

00:00:53.559 --> 00:00:58.450
is responsible for managing access to its
own resources, which are mostly files and

00:00:58.450 --> 00:00:59.450
folders.

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:03.200
On the other hand, a client-server network
is the type of network present in many medium

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:09.810
to large-sized businesses where a central
server hosts all the user accounts, devices,

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.970
and permissions for the users and devices.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:18.710
Active Directory, for example, is the server
piece in a Windows-based client-server network.

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:23.590
There are three data transmission types to
know about with networking: unicast, multicast,

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:24.799
and broadcast.

00:01:24.799 --> 00:01:28.729
In unicast, data is transmitted from one device
to another.

00:01:28.729 --> 00:01:33.030
With multicast, one device transmits data
to a selected group of devices.

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:37.980
In broadcast, a device transmits data to every
other device on its network.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.130
Finally, we have the network devices themselves.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.360
In this course, you will learn about switches,
routers, cables, and cable types.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:49.310
However, I want to highlight one type of device
now, and that is an Internet of Things or

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:50.910
IoT device.

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:55.370
An IoT device is a device that can be controlled
remotely, often through a smartphone app or

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:56.370
something similar.

00:01:56.370 --> 00:02:01.230
For example, a thermostat that can be programmed
remotely using an app is a prime example of

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:01.880
an IoT device.

